WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:02.800
Erica Wernick, Personal Coach, Author and
Speaker: The longer you’ve been in Hollywood

00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:09.599
the more rules you are trying to live by but
the thing is that for every rule in Hollywood

00:00:09.599 --> 00:00:11.490
there’s always an exception.

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:18.350
Film Courage: What if it’s been five or
ten years and someone’s not any closer to

00:00:18.350 --> 00:00:21.770
their quote unquote dream and they come here
from somewhere else?

00:00:21.770 --> 00:00:29.700
Erica: I think that question has nuance because
I’ve worked with a lot of people who have

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:35.490
been here for five or ten years who feel nowhere
near their dreams and who are really struggling

00:00:35.490 --> 00:00:42.710
but if we’re really honest it’s not like
absolutely nothing has happened in five or

00:00:42.710 --> 00:00:44.320
ten years.

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:47.239
Maybe they’re not where they want to be
yet, they haven’t booked that big TV show

00:00:47.239 --> 00:00:52.110
yet if they’re an actor or they haven’t
booked that big project (whatever their craft

00:00:52.110 --> 00:01:00.730
is) but I don’t think I’ve ever met any
artist out here who literally has not done

00:01:00.730 --> 00:01:05.750
a single thing in their career in five or
ten years unless they got a day job that became

00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:12.070
their sole priority and then they just worked
on let me get a promotion at my day job let

00:01:12.070 --> 00:01:23.090
me try to move up here and that 
becomes the priority over the dream and they

00:01:23.090 --> 00:01:28.270
kind of let the dream go for all those years
but of my clients that I've worked with I

00:01:28.270 --> 00:01:34.280
think it's important to celebrate what you
have achieved and to take like even if you're

00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:40.730
not where you want to be and it's been years
take a look at what you've actually done even

00:01:40.730 --> 00:01:48.590
if it feels small to you because I think that
if you can start shifting your focus onto

00:01:48.590 --> 00:01:53.229
what you have done instead of what you haven't
it'll help you feel more confident it'll help

00:01:53.229 --> 00:01:59.079
you feel you know just better energy you'll
feel proud of yourself you'll feel better

00:01:59.079 --> 00:02:06.189
and so I think that will lead to more things
happening now that being said you know when

00:02:06.189 --> 00:02:09.560
I work with people there's so many things
that we can take a look at it like you know

00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:15.800
why are you not where you want to be yet and
so we can take a look at what are the strategies

00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:22.390
that you either haven't tried at all or that
you have been procrastinating you know that's

00:02:22.390 --> 00:02:26.910
a big thing or that

00:02:26.910 --> 00:02:30.650
there's so many pieces sorry I feel like this
is gonna be a long answer but there's so many

00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:35.390
pieces to it it's like with the strategy piece
it's like are there things that you haven't

00:02:35.390 --> 00:02:41.370
tried and then if there are let's take a look
at why right and that all comes down to your

00:02:41.370 --> 00:02:45.630
belief system the fears that you have the
doubts that you have whether or not you believe

00:02:45.630 --> 00:02:51.090
in yourself and and that can get very nuanced
as well you know we get deep into what is

00:02:51.090 --> 00:02:56.790
really truly holding you back from doing some
of these strategic moves that you really haven't

00:02:56.790 --> 00:03:02.500
done yet and then another thing that I've
noticed with some of my clients is that the

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:08.099
longer you have been in the industry and I
always say Hollywood but I don't say Hollywood

00:03:08.099 --> 00:03:12.720
as in the location I call like the industry
Hollywood so the longer you've been in Hollywood

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:18.300
if you've been in Hollywood for five or ten
years you end up having more limiting beliefs

00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:24.030
than somebody who is brand new because someone
who is brand new they're so green and they're

00:03:24.030 --> 00:03:28.900
so naive that they take more risks because
they're not afraid of doing the wrong thing

00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:33.030
because they don't know what the wrong thing
is whereas someone who's been here for a long

00:03:33.030 --> 00:03:37.650
time and has been in the industry for a long
time you know more about how the industry

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:42.490
works and you might know more about like behind
the scenes and what relationships are important

00:03:42.490 --> 00:03:50.610
and what you need to be doing and then because
of that you you lose your sense of risk-taking

00:03:50.610 --> 00:03:57.610
and being bold and putting yourself out there
you know in a in a I hate to say in a risky

00:03:57.610 --> 00:04:01.710
way but it's all about taking you know it's
all about putting yourself out there and taking

00:04:01.710 --> 00:04:09.519
risks and so the longer you've been in Hollywood
the more rules you are trying to live by but

00:04:09.519 --> 00:04:17.299
the thing is is that for every rule in Hollywood
there's always an exception so you don't necessarily

00:04:17.299 --> 00:04:23.230
want to use these rules to hold you back and
when I say rules just for an example in the

00:04:23.230 --> 00:04:28.770
industry you know some of my actor clients
when they are wanting to if they need to get

00:04:28.770 --> 00:04:36.339
a new agent or a new manager one of the rules
in Hollywood is you should never contact representation

00:04:36.339 --> 00:04:40.740
and reach out to them during pilot season
right there's like certain times of the year

00:04:40.740 --> 00:04:46.159
that's busier maybe the beginning of episodic
or you know when pilot season is going on

00:04:46.159 --> 00:04:51.659
and you're not supposed to reach out right
and so it's just because you know how the

00:04:51.659 --> 00:04:55.410
industry works you've been in the industry
a long time you know that there's a pilot

00:04:55.410 --> 00:05:00.120
season you know that people are really busy
during pilot season but there's always going

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:06.199
to be an exception to that rule that rule
is not really set in stone and so if you follow

00:05:06.199 --> 00:05:10.539
that rule because you think you're you know
you have this knowledge about how the industry

00:05:10.539 --> 00:05:16.770
works and you want to be good and follow these
rules that you think you know in that process

00:05:16.770 --> 00:05:22.719
you are stopping yourself from taking a risk
and I've had many clients book agents during

00:05:22.719 --> 00:05:26.969
pilot season and then are able to go out for
pilot season so that's just a little example

00:05:26.969 --> 00:05:30.539
you know of I think some of the things that
hold people back in Hollywood the more they

00:05:30.539 --> 00:05:36.930
know the more it tends to hold them back and
then also with that there's a third part of

00:05:36.930 --> 00:05:44.270
this that I've really noticed with some of
my clients is that the more you know the more

00:05:44.270 --> 00:05:49.229
boxes you try to fit into and I feel like
that's just a big thing in Hollywood that

00:05:49.229 --> 00:05:54.879
a lot of people struggle no matter if you're
a writer a director you know working behind

00:05:54.879 --> 00:06:00.460
the scenes somehow an actor you know even
my production designer boss couple years ago

00:06:00.460 --> 00:06:05.060
fell into that of like how can I you know
oh I do drama and they don't think I can do

00:06:05.060 --> 00:06:12.279
comedy and so I have found that a lot of people
get stuck in these boxes because they're trusting

00:06:12.279 --> 00:06:20.360
other people to tell them what box they fit
into you know like with actors it all all

00:06:20.360 --> 00:06:26.210
starts in the beginning with headshots I've
seen this so many times where an actor is

00:06:26.210 --> 00:06:31.490
told to put their headshot on facebook and
ask everyone on facebook what what roles can

00:06:31.490 --> 00:06:36.199
you see me as with this headshot you know
what shows do you see me in according to this

00:06:36.199 --> 00:06:41.279
headshot and that's a super common thing right
and if they're not asking their facebook friends

00:06:41.279 --> 00:06:46.089
they're asking their agents their managers
their you know their teachers but every time

00:06:46.089 --> 00:06:53.119
you do that you are asking somebody else to
tell you how to live your dream and you're

00:06:53.119 --> 00:06:58.179
letting somebody else dictate what roles you
go out for what projects you get to be a part

00:06:58.179 --> 00:07:04.270
of and you completely lose your voice and
so I think that when people struggle in the

00:07:04.270 --> 00:07:09.219
five to ten year range I think that that sometimes
depending on the person you know plays a part

00:07:09.219 --> 00:07:15.559
where all these years maybe they've been trying
to pursue the industry in these boxes that

00:07:15.559 --> 00:07:20.849
they were told they should be in but it never
feels good and it never feels aligned and

00:07:20.849 --> 00:07:24.979
so they struggle you know I have one of my
clients really struggle with that and you

00:07:24.979 --> 00:07:28.770
know she's done such beautiful work as we
work together for the past two years and she's

00:07:28.770 --> 00:07:35.270
really come into her own right like you know
she's really connected with who she really

00:07:35.270 --> 00:07:39.589
is now and who she wants to be and not who
Hollywood has been telling her to be all these

00:07:39.589 --> 00:07:44.860
years and then that leads to more exciting
opportunities because you're more confident

00:07:44.860 --> 00:07:51.919
and standing on in your own more often and
you know that will bleed into the work that

00:07:51.919 --> 00:07:56.879
you do even though it sounds counterintuitive
like oh erica but Hollywood loves the boxes

00:07:56.879 --> 00:08:02.729
stepping out of that is somehow going to help
you succeed I think yes I think it's about

00:08:02.729 --> 00:08:07.719
alignment and and being yourself a hundred
percent I think that will always lead to more

00:08:07.719 --> 00:08:14.960
success I love what you're saying what if
what if we can't accurately see ourselves

00:08:14.960 --> 00:08:20.099
because like I knew someone who was was auditioning
and she was doing well and she was about to

00:08:20.099 --> 00:08:25.589
turn 30 which when you look back at it from
an older age is not old at all but a friend

00:08:25.589 --> 00:08:30.779
or so she thought said oh let me see your
headshots and so she showed this friend and

00:08:30.779 --> 00:08:36.250
the friend said oh and he gave her this feedback
he said look you're getting near 30 so you're

00:08:36.250 --> 00:08:43.089
going to want to aim for mom rules so maybe
tone down the sexy factor a little bit and

00:08:43.089 --> 00:08:47.269
that was sort of eye-opening to this person
because they still saw themselves as this

00:08:47.269 --> 00:08:53.480
you know not sex kitten but they just saw
themselves as not as the mom and so i'm just

00:08:53.480 --> 00:08:57.860
wondering how much is it that we're blinded
by ourselves because we can't really see ourselves

00:08:57.860 --> 00:09:04.870
accurately sometimes or was she allowing this
person to really tell her how to be yeah I

00:09:04.870 --> 00:09:09.779
think I mean it's a great question and and
i'm sure that you know every agent or casting

00:09:09.779 --> 00:09:17.389
director or whatever will disagree with me
but I think that the more we buy into the

00:09:17.389 --> 00:09:24.759
boxes the more we are helping perpetuate the
problem and so I think that you know with

00:09:24.759 --> 00:09:31.209
the rise of streamers like netflix and hulu
and amazon we are getting to see more complex

00:09:31.209 --> 00:09:36.360
stories which is amazing because with the
networks of abc nbc you know those are likely

00:09:36.360 --> 00:09:40.779
following formulas that have worked before
and they're a little more of stereotype when

00:09:40.779 --> 00:09:46.900
it comes to characters and content and you
know and story but because there's so much

00:09:46.900 --> 00:09:51.639
more content now available with the streaming
I really do feel that we are finally moving

00:09:51.639 --> 00:09:58.120
into an area of Hollywood where we are getting
more complex storytelling and so to me like

00:09:58.120 --> 00:10:04.550
the mom right you turn 30 and then you need
to play the mom it's like could you be a super

00:10:04.550 --> 00:10:11.350
complex character that happens to have a child
right and so I think that we need to stop

00:10:11.350 --> 00:10:17.810
letting Hollywood define us as the mom even
if that feels hard you know even if you feel

00:10:17.810 --> 00:10:22.339
like you're getting the advice from someone
trusted who's been in the industry I think

00:10:22.339 --> 00:10:30.220
it is partly on us to stop perpetuating those
kinds of things that happen you know like

00:10:30.220 --> 00:10:34.879
the client that I was talking about she just
came out as non-binary and that can be really

00:10:34.879 --> 00:10:39.930
scary as an actor you know because she's having
to explore with her representation what that

00:10:39.930 --> 00:10:45.519
means in terms of what roles she wants to
go out for but to her for her to even have

00:10:45.519 --> 00:10:52.410
that conversation with her representation
she is breaking through the the boxes and

00:10:52.410 --> 00:10:57.839
the Hollywood stuff you know of stereotypes
and she is challenging it because she is saying

00:10:57.839 --> 00:11:04.709
this is who I am this is who I know that I
am and so let's explore what kind of complex

00:11:04.709 --> 00:11:10.670
roles I can play so I guess to answer your
question about you know finding ourselves

00:11:10.670 --> 00:11:17.480
if we feel like maybe we've lost ourselves
I think you have to begin by tuning out everything

00:11:17.480 --> 00:11:22.529
tuning out the noise don't listen to me don't
listen to other people you have to get back

00:11:22.529 --> 00:11:30.350
to yourself and what your dream truly is you
know the very first chapter of my book I invite

00:11:30.350 --> 00:11:35.570
people to write down everything they would
love to happen in their career everything

00:11:35.570 --> 00:11:41.439
that you dream of you know get back to that
dream you had in your heart when you were

00:11:41.439 --> 00:11:46.319
a kid or whenever it was that you decided
you wanted to pursue this dream get back to

00:11:46.319 --> 00:11:52.060
that and connect to that and really you know
some of my clients too they'll they'll study

00:11:52.060 --> 00:11:56.430
they'll do some research and watch you know
some shows that they really love and start

00:11:56.430 --> 00:12:00.759
to explore what excites them you know when
you watch this do you feel really excited

00:12:00.759 --> 00:12:07.689
because you'd love to play a complex role
like this person so I think that process involves

00:12:07.689 --> 00:12:14.189
you know you reconnecting with your own intuition
and tuning out the noise and get really true

00:12:14.189 --> 00:12:23.589
to who you are and decide that you are worthy
and are capable of succeeding as who you are

00:12:23.589 --> 00:12:27.570
even if others tell you no you need to play
the mom or know you need to fit in this box.

00:12:27.570 --> 00:12:27.820
What was the most helpful part of this video
for you?

